{
  "image_name": "1a98aa293f83a5068d904e923f3afe4a.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how the immunoglobulin heavy-chain (Igh) locus is organized and how V(D)J recombination is directed. Across the top, a long chromosomal map runs from telomere to centromere, showing many variable (V_H) gene segments spread over megabases, followed by diversity (D_H), joining (J_H), and constant-region genes. Colored symbols indicate recombination signal sequences (12-RSS and 23-RSS) and CTCF-binding elements, highlighting that the locus is highly structured rather than a simple linear array.\n\nThe central mechanistic point is the “12/23 rule.” One panel compares 12-RSS and 23-RSS motifs, and another shows that V, D, and J segments recombine in the permitted order because only compatible RSS pairs synapse. A cleavage complex containing RAG1 and RAG2 cuts at the RSSs, then classical non-homologous end joining joins the coding ends, producing a D–J or V–DJ exon while the RSS ends form a signal joint circle.\n\nThe final schematic emphasizes 3D genome architecture: CTCF and cohesin appear to loop and compact the Igh locus, bringing distant V segments toward the recombination center near the J_H region. Overall, the image links locus topology, RSS compatibility, and RAG-mediated cleavage to ordered antibody gene assembly.",
